'''winter-hall†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wyntyr-halle'', ''wyntirhaule''; < ''winter''<sup>1</sup> + ''hall''.] A hall used especially in winter.
{{smaller block|The utmost Chambur nexte ''Winter Halle''.
{{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 486.}}
A ''wyntir haule'', hibernium, hibernaculum, hiemaculum.
{{right|''Cath. Ang.'', p. 420.}}}}

'''winter-house†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wyntyr-howse''; < ''winter''<sup>1</sup> + ''house''<sup>1</sup>.] A house used especially in winter.
{{smaller block|''Wyntyr howse'' or halle . . . Hibernaculum.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 530.}}}}

'''winteridge''' (win′tėr-ij), ''n.'' [For *''winterage'', < ''winter''<sup>1</sup> + ''-age''.] Winter food for cattle. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''wintering''' (win′tėr-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''winter''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of one who or that which winters in a specified place or manner.
{{smaller block|If God so prosper your voyage that you may . . . obtaine from him [the Prince of Cathay] his letters of priuiledge against the next yeeres spring, you may then . . . search and discouer somewhat further then you had discouered before your ''wintering''. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 434.}}}}
'''2.''' Provision of fodder, shelter, etc., for cattle during winter.
{{smaller block|Young lean cattle may by their growth pay for their ''wintering'', and so be ready to fat next summer.
{{right|''Mortimer'', Husbandry.}}}}

'''winterish''' (win′tėr-ish), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''wynterysshe''; < ''winter''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Of or pertaining to winter; wintry.
{{smaller block|''Wynterysshe'', belonging to the wynter.
{{right|''Palsgrave'', p. 329.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''wintry''' (win′tri), ''a.'' [Also ''wintery''; < ME. *''wintry'', < AS. ''wintrig'', ''wintreg'' (cf. G. ''winterieht''); as ''winter''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Of or pertaining to winter; occurring in winter; peculiar or appropriate to the cold season of the year; cold and stormy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ere the clouds gather, and the ''wint’ry'' sky
Descends in storms to intercept our passage.</poem>
{{right|''Rowe'', Jane Shore, ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|Great ice-crystals . . . gave the vessel a ''wintery'' appearance. {{right|''C. F. Hall'', Polar Expedition, 1876, p. 415.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, cool; chilly; frosty.
{{smaller block|She could even smile — a faint, sweet, ''wintery'' smile.
{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Cranford, ii.}}}}

'''winy''' (wī′ni), ''a.'' [< ''wine'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Characteristic of or peculiar to wine; resembling wine; pertaining to or influenced by wine; vinous. Also ''winey''.
{{smaller block|But, being once well chafed with wine, . . . there was no matter their ears had ever heard of that grew not to be a subject of their ''winie'' conference.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|They are much like such Grapes as grow on our Vines, both in shape and colour; and they are of a very pleasant ''Winy'' taste. {{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, I. 392.}}}}

'''winze'''<sup>1</sup> (winz), ''n.'' [Prob. < *''winze'', ''v.'', winnow, Icel. ''vinza'', winnow, < ''vindr'', wind: see ''wind''<sup>2</sup>, and cf. ''winnow''.] In ''mining'', a vertical or inclined excavation which is like a shaft except that it does not rise to the surface. {{smaller|The winze}}
